## The question: where does pay variation actually sit?

### A raw look, discipline by discipline

```{r raw-look}
disc_summary <- graduates |>
  group_by(Discipline) |>
  summarise(
    n_majors = n(),
    mean_salary = mean(Median_salary),
    sd_salary = sd(Median_salary)
  ) |>
  arrange(desc(mean_salary))

disc_summary
```

```{r raw-plot}
graduates |>
  mutate(Discipline = fct_reorder(Discipline, Median_salary, .fun = median)) |>
  ggplot(aes(x = Median_salary, y = Discipline)) +
  geom_boxplot(fill = "#122a52", alpha = 0.7) +
  geom_jitter(height = 0.15, alpha = 0.4, colour = "#d32f2f") +
  labs(
    title = "Median salary by major, grouped by discipline",
    subtitle = "Each red point is one major; boxes summarise its discipline",
    x = "Median salary (USD)", y = NULL
  )
```

There's an obvious question sitting in this plot: some of the spread is
*between* disciplines (Engineering majors, as a group, sit higher than
Education majors) and some is *within* a discipline (majors inside
Engineering still differ from each other). **How much of the total
variance is which?**

