## Where this kind of analysis has to be handled carefully

::: callout-important
## This is not, on its own, a pay equity audit

A variance-components model tells you *how much* variance sits at a
given level of structure — it does not, by itself, tell you whether
that structure is fair, legal, or free of bias. A real pay equity
analysis needs to control for legitimate, defensible factors (role,
experience, performance, market rate) before looking at protected
characteristics, typically involves legal counsel, and often should be
conducted under legal privilege. Treat everything in this chapter as
the statistical building block, not the whole analysis — and bring in
the right specialists (legal, compensation, sometimes external counsel)
before this kind of model output goes anywhere near a real pay
decision.
:::

::: callout-tip
## Try it yourself

Add `Unemployment_rate` as a fixed-effect predictor alongside
`(1 | Discipline)`. Does the discipline-level variance shrink once
you account for it? What would that tell you about *why* disciplines
differ in pay?
:::

